ase it's usually a wiki  although we're slowly moving a lot of  that stuff to google docs  um and then we define it there open it  up for people it's not closed it's not  secret  and then if anybody has an objection or  has some input they're  um they're encouraged to present that  whether that's cto or just another  engineer  but it's really an organization where  it's about  do don't ask um which helps  if it was a little bit more a structured  hierarchy then  or a little bit more micromanaging then  it would be a lot harder because then  you have even in  a fairly flat organization we would  still have to talk to the chapter lead  then the tribe lead and then the cto  instead we just kind of do that and you  know it's much easier to ask for  forgiveness and permission um and nobody  really calls us  out on whether or not you should have  done that  okay so how do you know that  what you're doing is actually better or  worse  than the more traditional approaches  located so the question is how do we  know what's better if it's better  it's a really hard question just like  anything else when it comes to crew  development because there's  the metrics are pretty scarce one metric  is we do a lot of employee happiness  um so every quarter we do we do a study  and we we talk to people and we you know  sometimes it's part survey part  communication part talking  and some of those metrics on that is how  are you how are you feeling about your  career development so to be honest the  fact that the add-ons hasn't haven't  been working and haven't been taken off  showed up in these reports and what we  saw was that our scores in that area of  career development were actually  lower than they had been in a long time  and that's what started us leading  along the lines of do we need this  career ladder in addition to the add-ons  do we need this  individual contributor ladder of senior  developer principal developer what have  you  and that's formed a working group so  that 