e  going to fail at the mission of whatever  that mission is it could be the greatest  product in the world  and you're not going to get it so i  think those are kind of i think  at the end of it it might just be the  sheer success or failure of spotify  it's it's not negative or positive so  the feedback is kind of  oh those exist it's really a knowledge  gap right so when i started  working with engineers i would talk we  would they would hear about add-ons and  they'd be like well how do i do that  and just the sheer amount of effort to  like find information about it so this  goes to the selling  even a great idea i think poorly sold is  a failure  and i think that some of that failure to  sell it  was part of it now if we actually had  everybody that knew about add-ons and  people were unhappy with it i'd be more  than happy to walk away from and say  look  great experiment we learned something  move on but where i came in is  i still think the idea is solid and  nobody half the people or more than half  the people in the organization  don't even know they exist which is not  a good place to be so part of what i  wanted it was to correct some of the  things that  we saw as problematic and then we're  going to go and  this road manager idea of like and let's  actually talk about this with real  people  let's sit down with teams let's stand  with managers and and chat about it  to get that feedback going um because we  could be completely off  in left field  oh yeah so i have a question about this  idea of the matrix where you have  self-directed teams but then you have  chapter leads  how do you guys protect against chapter  leads from having like  uh i guess undue influence on how the  team works by being able to  affect multiple teams across the board  so the question is  um i think boils down to whenever you  look at a matrix model the fear is  oh wow now the people have two bosses  and that is a distinct fear  so part of it is hiring the right  chapter leads