ng  with people i mean  as you can imagine this process is not  cheap from a labor standpoint  is we try to keep chapters about 10 to  12 in some cases they get a little bit  bigger  so as you can imagine with 600 engineers  there's multiple back end chapters  that's where the guild process comes in  so the guild is basically the idea this  you have these chapters that should be  small enough to be functional teams and  small enough that the manager has time  with each  person but shouldn't be but we still  need that commonality across  the space and so as a guild we'll run  things like on conferences where we'll  gather up once or twice a year  as an entire group the one we just did  in new york was about 100 100 people of  back-end engineers  and we met up at the ace hotel and spent  a day just talking about back-end  engineering  and another day talking about data  processing and data pipelines  with about 80 people so those those  sorts of things are there  but we try to keep it at 10 to 12. so  it's a system that's not optimized for  cost and again it gets back to the  the fact that we have the money to grow  but it's really optimized for  speed of the product and getting things  out there and um  the development of of the engineers and  individual contributors  you seem to describe somewhat of a  static situation in your slides i'm  curious how  uh how things grow so blackberry comes  to life  tomorrow and then suddenly it's 10  million users  how do you spell the blackberry team and  how do you  organize the people around them so the  question is about how do we spin up to  kind of  uh be more dynamic and and be able to  maneuver a large company so um  part of it is having a cross-functional  team so we don't have a blackberry team  or an ios team or  an android team we used to to be honest  we used to have those teams  and we decided to base it around feature  set so for  in your example if blackberry also  became a player and we wanted to be able  to  to su