h him and he  started doing more android development  and getting android projects so it was  kind of a slow rollout  and now we're at that stage where he  really enjoys it and we're starting to  roll him over to the mobile chapter so  it wasn't just a  oh you want to do mobile development not  my problem here go go forth and and  go bother him he said it was a much more  organic slow growth thing  and then today i have a front-end web  developer who's really much more of a  full stack developer and has been  concentrating much more on the data  pipeline stuff so we had a conversation  today of how can he start exploring  joining our chapter starting to  participate with our group  without having to go all in and so  that'll be another slow roll out  probably over a couple of months  in the back  you know something that was chosen to be  developed how was it chosen  to build this new thing how's the  decision made  so yeah the question revolves around how  do you have a self-managing team but  still  kind of meet the mission so part of it  is like  resisting the urge to micromanage so it  usually starts with an initiative from  daniel akus who's the ceo of spotify  who may say something as broad as hey we  want a  this is this is part of a true story of  we want to offer a mobile free tier so  up until november of last year if you  were on mobile you had to pay  there was uh and that was just part of  it  um he wanted to be able to have a mobile  free tier and as you can imagine that's  wide reaching there's lots of people  that need to be involved in that so what  happened at that is that rolled down to  the next  set of tier where design had to start  figuring out what would that look like  the legal team had to start working up  on contracts the  labor relations people had to start  reaching out to the labels and say what  does this look like how do you need how  does this structure work for you and  work for us  and then from a technology standpoint we  have to 