es this organizational structure  influence our architecture quite a bit  so we have tried to structure our  architecture to achieve that autonomy so  it's micro services on the back end  um we have basically the at the bottom  tier a micro service  the tier right above that and then we  have an aggregation service so that the  micro services themselves will never  communicate with one another  so we don't get into these whole like  you know one call results in 47  different things  then we have an aggregation layer so  that the client app we kind of came from  this model where  the application would make multiple like  most ajaxi type of things would like  you'd hit a page and maybe there'd be 47  calls to the site to get all the assets  and we said that's not great so let's  just do one hit so that's where  aggregation services kind of came into  play  so the aggregation services are owned by  a team like the views are owned by a  team but the infrastructure is owned  by all of us the micro services are  usually owned by a team  and then the other part of it is on the  client app side we have  squads that we call container squads and  what their idea is is build the platform  so build the thing that you plug your  future into to make sure things like  build processes are stable to make sure  that the  common things that you need to do are  taken care of make sure  you know they're the ones working with  apple to get things approved and working  with  the android store they're working with  all of those things to provide  our future teams a place to put their  feature and then the future teams really  own their feature  you know and that's a work in progress  with ios it's pretty  it's in one place and with android is in  a different place and they both have  their strength and weaknesses android is  much further along  and they're more continuous deployment  or continuous delivery type of model  whereas um ios is much more structured  around like the development p